# Const Arrow Functions

Declare functions as `const` arrow functions, not `function` declarations. This
applies to module-level helpers, exported functions, and React components.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
export function cardKey(name: string): string {
  return stripVersion(name).toLowerCase();
}

async function fetchDoc(url: string): Promise<Document> { /* ... */ }

export function App() {
  return <div />;
}

// ✅ GOOD
export const cardKey = (name: string): string => stripVersion(name).toLowerCase();

const fetchDoc = async (url: string): Promise<Document> => { /* ... */ };

export const App = () => {
  return <div />;
};
```

## Exception: functions that rely on `this`

Keep a regular `function` when the function's behavior depends on runtime `this`
binding, since arrow functions capture `this` lexically and would break it. This
covers prototype method patches and reassigned host methods:

```typescript
// ✅ Must stay a regular function — `this` is the XHR instance / window.
OriginalXHR.prototype.open = function open(this: XMLHttpRequest, method: string) {
  return originalOpen.call(this, method);
};

window.fetch = async function patchedFetch(this: typeof window, input: RequestInfo) {
  return originalFetch.call(this, input);
};
```

## Notes

- Arrow consts are not hoisted, so define a function before the module-level code
  that calls it. (Calls made later at runtime, e.g. from other functions, are fine.)
- Class methods stay as methods — this rule is about standalone functions.
